 [17592186110392 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2604" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110392 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/86/867b3664e93f42c55a2f97dc28bf66794484e226981632c85f725926cec77def.png)\n\nWhat type of angle is $\\angle RST,$ shown in the figure above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110392 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "154c65b2-4d51-5da0-b00c-293f3fba3bd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110392 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the vertex of $\\angle RST$ is point $S,$ not point $T.$ So $\\angle RST$ is *not* a straight angle.\n\nAs a result, the two rays $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{SR}$ and $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{ST}$ of our angle coincide. So, we can think of $\\angle RST$ as:\n\n- A *null* angle, assuming it doesn't turn at all.\n\n![](images/a8/a8916a724d4463bf2047c919b73770160d3124838b61af0dcdc2b9e04322381a.png)\n\n- A *full* angle, assuming it makes one full turn.\n\n![](images/9c/9cd768b4361e95a18ffa52d6315fb5ea6cd0cf6784bc4c9e12b1bae1f6566755.png)\n\nSo, the answer is \"Null\" or \"Full.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110672 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110672 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Right Angles" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110672 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110672 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6d203cc5-4e76-5f78-b431-b357164de3f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110673 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110924 #edb/ref 17592186045417